00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.560
And there we go.

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:12.740
Brooklyn has the most number of
parties, followed by Manhattan,

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.120
followed by Queens.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:19.250
And we saw how easy it was to go
from a data set to do a query,

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.925
and to go ahead and display
it using Data Studio.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:28.150
So what was
different about this?

00:03:28.150 --> 00:03:29.960
And it's kind of good
to step back and say,

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:31.251
OK, what exactly was different?

00:03:31.251 --> 00:03:36.250
So sure, I showed you going to a
website, running the SQL query,

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.110
and then going to
another website

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:41.350
and visualizing the data
all in one workflow.

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:42.490
So, great.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:44.050
But the key thing
that's different

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:49.930
here is that the data didn't
actually move anywhere.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:51.910
The data was there in BigQuery.

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.860
It was there on
the Google Cloud.

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.250
And what we did was that
we brought a whole bunch

00:03:57.250 --> 00:03:59.810
of compute to this data.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:01.814
So if you were to go
to this data set--

00:04:01.814 --> 00:04:03.480
so let's go ahead and
view the data set.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.280
And let's see.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.860
There is a 311 service request.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.440
And look at the details
of this data set.

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.300
That was 8, almost
9 gigs of data

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:23.150
that we queried in
a matter of seconds.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.740
How is that possible?

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.810
The only reason
it's possible was

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.940
because we threw thousands of
machines on this one SQL query

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:33.240
to execute it.

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:34.970
And there was no way
that this data was

00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.490
present on those compute nodes.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:41.210
Instead, just like these
birds flocking over a sea,

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.140
we brought a bunch of machines
to go ahead and process

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:49.190
the data in a distributed form,
and give us the answer back.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.040
So if you were to think of
what the old world was in terms

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.590
of data analysis,
the old world was

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:59.120
to go ahead and download data to
your laptop, analyze the data,

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.170
and then distribute the results.

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.370
But that old world doesn't
really work anymore.

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:07.160
And the reason it doesn't
work is that the kind of data

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.702
sets that we are now
dealing with are much, much,

00:05:09.702 --> 00:05:11.210
much larger.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:12.920
If you take an
industrial use case,

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:16.220
you're probably producing
anywhere from half a terabyte

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.230
to 4 terabytes per
machine per year.

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.700
Or health care, a typical
hospital of more than a

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.140
terabyte per month.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.800
Or transportation, you have
about 2 gigs of data a year.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:30.200
And if you're in retail,
you may be collecting up

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.840
to 4 gigs of data per
day per product line.

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.830
This is a lot of data.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:37.150
And when you think
of that kind of data,

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.690
this old workflow of where you
start with downloading data,

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:45.080
it doesn't work anymore,
because data doesn't fit.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.120
The other thing that
happens is that when

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.220
you want to do your
processing, there

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:53.600
is a lot more compute power
available on the cloud.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.080
And especially when it gets into
things like machine learning.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.840
What's shown here is a
Tensor Processing Unit--

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.080
a TPU-- a custom ASIC
for machine learning.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.660
And if you want to do machine
learning on large data sets,

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:08.450
and do them in a
fast way, you need

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:11.700
to be able to access
hardware like this.

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:16.622
And at that point, thinking of
analyzing data on your laptop,

00:06:16.622 --> 00:06:18.080
it doesn't really
work, because you

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:22.520
need lots of compute to be
able to do your analysis,

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:25.120
to do-- especially a
machine learning-- on data

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.510
sets this large.

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:29.840
And the third aspect of this
is that in the old world

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:31.850
you would distribute reports.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.400
It was OK if you just
give somebody a PDF.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:38.510
But these days, everyone is
used to interactive reports.

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:41.980
They want to be able to do
what I just did in Data Studio,

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:45.140
or what you do when
somebody sends you a map.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.050
They don't send
you a PDF of a map.

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.300
They send you a Google
Map that you can then

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:53.120
go ahead and interact with.

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.130
And that's true of charts.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:56.840
That's true of documents.

00:06:56.840 --> 00:06:58.910
People are used to
interactive reports.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.120
And so when you distribute the
results for your data analysis,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:04.600
they need to be interactive.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:08.260
And so the old world where
you downloaded the data,

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:11.410
analyze the data on your
laptop, and then email

00:07:11.410 --> 00:07:15.620
your results as a PDF,
those don't work anymore.

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.070
So the new world
is a world in which

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:22.080
you need to analyze, share
your data, and do your reports.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.750
And so what I just did in that
demo of finding the boroughs

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:30.070
in New York with the most
number of parties, that

00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.060
illustrated this new world--

00:07:32.060 --> 00:07:35.230
this new world where
the data stays in place,

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:37.360
and you bring your
analysis to it.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.870
And that is what public data
sets on Google Cloud are about.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:44.140
It's about the data
staying in place,

00:07:44.140 --> 00:07:47.860
and being able to do massive
amounts of compute on the data,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:52.690
and to be able to provide
the results of that analysis

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:58.060
in a form that your end users
can still play around with,

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:02.820
can interact with, can
query it themselves as well.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:06.830
So the whole idea here is that
we started with a data set.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.920
And having gotten
the data set, we

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:18.140
went ahead and
clicked on the query.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:20.060
And then we ran the query.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:22.700
And then we clicked on
exploring Data Studio.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:25.040
And then I was in that
interactive report.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.940
And I was able to go ahead
and change the way I displayed

00:08:28.940 --> 00:08:30.500
the data and I did the data.

00:08:30.500 --> 00:08:32.480
And all of this is possible.

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:35.059
And with Data Studio I
can share these things.

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:38.000
And it's all possible because
it's all on the cloud,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.860
and the data remains in place.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.340
I mean, there's no way you can
think of moving 9 gigs of data

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:46.490
around every time you want
to do analysis like this.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.890
The data needs to stay in place.

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.020
And you need to bring
compute to the data.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.840
But this is not
just about analysis.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.740
So what I just showed you
with parties in New York

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.460
was about analysis.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:03.520
But what's a new cool thing?

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:04.810
Machine learning.

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.130
And so even in BigQuery,
right, with our data sets,

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:12.520
we can actually train a
machine learning model.

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.660
And this time I'll pick a
data set also from New York

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:18.370
to predict a taxi fare.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:21.850
So given the pickup location
and the drop-off location,

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:26.050
I would like to basically figure
out what would the taxi cost.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:28.450
And in order to do that,
I will go back and look

00:09:28.450 --> 00:09:31.570
at historical data of
all kinds of taxi trips,

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:36.190
and what was the actual
amount paid by passengers

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:37.724
taking those taxi trips.

00:09:37.724 --> 00:09:39.640
And this will allow me
to demonstrate BigQuery

00:09:39.640 --> 00:09:42.460
ML, and also BigQuery GIS.

00:09:42.460 --> 00:09:44.650
Because this is a
special data set.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:47.320
People get picked up at
specific locations, dropped off

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:48.760
at specific locations.

00:09:48.760 --> 00:09:51.580
And rather than do it
from the user interface--

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.640
BigQuery user
interface-- this time

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:56.080
I'll do it from a
Python notebook.

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:58.240
And I'll also show
you another cool tool

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.370
called a geomapper or Geo Viz.

00:10:01.370 --> 00:10:03.140
So going down here.

00:10:03.140 --> 00:10:05.530
So this is a GitHub
repository that

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:07.630
contains a taxi fare model.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.190
So this is a Python notebook.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.970
I just loaded it into a Datalab,
which is a Python notebook

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.020
environment.

00:10:14.020 --> 00:10:17.230
I can go ahead and, in
Datalab, I can click on a cell,

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.700
and I can run the cell.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.350
And this allows me to run it.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.960
And let's see.

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:26.710
So we can go down here,
do the Python imports.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:30.910
And here now is
my BigQuery query.

00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:33.550
So the key part of
this query is that I'm

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:38.290
selecting some number of rows
from a table of New York City

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:39.520
taxi trips.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.810
So I can go ahead and run that.

00:10:41.810 --> 00:10:45.850
And then I can say, go ahead
and execute this query.

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:47.950
And I get 1,000 of them.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:53.230
And I can look at
the top few records.

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:55.150
And there's my trips.head.

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:58.270
And so on.

00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:03.670
And so we can go ahead and
look at the description

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:05.250
of those things.

00:11:05.250 --> 00:11:09.140
But that is, at this point,
just still data analysis.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.410
So let's look at this line.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:14.330
This is the line that
does the machine learning.

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:17.470
So I take your query
that pulls in records.

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.750
And in this case,
I'm pulling records

00:11:19.750 --> 00:11:22.720
that contain the hour of the
day, the pick-up longitude,

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:25.000
the pick-up latitude,
the drop-off

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:27.700
longitude, the drop-off
latitude, all of which

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:28.960
come from the data.

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:31.440
And the total fare
that was paid.

00:11:31.440 --> 00:11:33.040
And that also comes
from the data--

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.130
the total fare being
the actual fair amount

00:11:36.130 --> 00:11:38.540
plus any tolls, et cetera.

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:40.330
So those are the columns.

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:44.080
And I say, please go ahead and
create a machine-learning model

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:49.420
to predict the taxi fare using
a linear regression model.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:51.520
And the rest of these
things are optional.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:52.960
So we can do that.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.900
And at this point
we can go ahead

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:58.360
and say, run this query for me.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.730
I don't want to overwrite
the query I already have.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.590
So I'll call this Demos2.

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:05.056
And I'll go ahead and
say, run the query.

00:12:05.056 --> 00:12:07.660
Oh, I need to run
this [? cell, ?]

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:09.590
and then run the query.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:14.170
And we can then go ahead
and execute the query.

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:16.550
And this takes about
5 to 10 minutes.

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:18.520
So I'll let that execute.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:20.470
And I'll assume that
it's already done.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:24.580
And I'll just move on to what
you would get at the end of it.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:28.570
So after that, what you will
get is that in BigQuery there

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:31.960
will be a model that's created
that is capable of doing

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:32.740
this prediction.

00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.810
And notice that all
that we really did

00:12:34.810 --> 00:12:36.670
was that we took
a normal BigQuery

00:12:36.670 --> 00:12:41.170
query that did the standard
SQL and added three lines.

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:44.470
Go ahead and create the model
with this particular algorithm

00:12:44.470 --> 00:12:48.250
to predict this column based
on all these other columns.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.810
And at that point, we also
get back the training loss.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:55.660
As the error decreases
as the model trains,

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:59.450
we can evaluate it on
another independent data set.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.510
So this is just
another BigQuery query

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:06.610
where I'm doing a select
from an ML that evaluate.

00:13:06.610 --> 00:13:09.760
And then I can go ahead
and look at the errors.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:14.110
And the errors turn out
to be about $6 on average.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:17.200
And then we can also go
ahead and evaluate it

00:13:17.200 --> 00:13:20.530
based on, OK, so we have
$6 on average error.

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:23.080
But does the error
change depending

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:25.090
on where people were
picked up, or where

00:13:25.090 --> 00:13:26.570
people were dropped off?

00:13:26.570 --> 00:13:29.920
And so what I can do is that
I can go ahead and select

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.270
the total fare.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:37.450
I can go ahead and find
the error and group it

00:13:37.450 --> 00:13:41.380
by dollars, group it
by the amount of fare.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:45.210
And if I did that, I
would find that the errors

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:49.390
are really small when
the fair is around $11.

00:13:49.390 --> 00:13:52.360
And it increases
quadratically on each side.

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:55.480
So it basically, on
very expensive trips,

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:58.040
and very cheap trips,
the errors are very high.

00:13:58.040 --> 00:14:01.220
And so this is, well, we
need to do something else.

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:06.610
So what I'll do is I'll go
ahead and add the ST underscore

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.860
geographic point, which is
a spatial query in BigQuery.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:12.640
So this allows me to basically
treat the pick-up longitude

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.980
and pick-up latitude not
as two independent points,

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:17.590
but at one point--

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:18.910
a pick-up point.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:21.490
And having gotten
that pick-up point,

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:27.275
then I will go ahead and
compute the distance--

00:14:27.275 --> 00:14:31.660
ST_Distance-- between the
pickup and drop-off point.

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:35.990
And I'll use that as one
of my training variables.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:38.710
So even though this is not
present in the original data,

00:14:38.710 --> 00:14:41.410
I'm now using some
geospatial functions

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:45.430
to go ahead and
create a new feature.

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.610
And when I do that, long
story short, my errors

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:52.810
become very flat across
all of these dollar ranges.

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:55.690
So I'm actually learning
to predict much better.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:59.620
And then let's go
ahead and visualize it.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:04.090
And let's now let's look
at how these errors change

00:15:04.090 --> 00:15:05.930
based on location.

00:15:05.930 --> 00:15:07.870
So what I'm going
to do is that I'm

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:12.700
going to basically take this
query, which what it does

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:17.830
is that it groups these
things by drop-off grid point.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:22.110
And I'll go to the
BigQuery Geo Viz,

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:26.750
and put my project ID in there.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:30.946
And say that I want
to run that query.

00:15:30.946 --> 00:15:36.810
[INAUDIBLE] And run that query.

00:15:46.091 --> 00:15:46.590
OK.

00:15:46.590 --> 00:15:47.840
So there it is.

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:49.930
So I see the results.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:52.440
And I'll change it to
the drop-off grid point.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:54.570
And I see my results here.

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:56.910
Let me go ahead and
change the style,

00:15:56.910 --> 00:16:01.400
and change the fill color to
be data-driven, and make it

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:03.250
a linear interval.

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:10.020
And let's go ahead and do
the range of that color.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:13.810
And maybe from 0 to 20.

00:16:13.810 --> 00:16:18.010
And the field could be
the mean absolute error.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:19.330
And let's go ahead and do that.

00:16:19.330 --> 00:16:22.480
And you see that the
colors are different.

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.740
But at this point,
my original data,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:27.660
the resolution is
actually pretty small,

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:31.480
because I did resolution
of 0.1 degrees.

00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:34.630
Let me change that
to 0.01 degrees.

00:16:34.630 --> 00:16:36.990
So now I get a lot more points.

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.390
I can go back to my style.

00:16:39.390 --> 00:16:41.220
And there we go.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:42.710
And as you can see--

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:45.190
let me change the
color a little bit.

00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:50.860
Code.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:55.210
So you can see that the
errors are low in Manhattan.

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:57.580
And as you go further
and further away

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:00.160
into Brooklyn, et cetera,
the errors increase.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:02.140
And this is a kind
of visualization

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.780
that we did relatively
quickly on the map.

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:10.540
So let's move back here.

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:11.740
So, cool.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:12.550
That was great.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:14.770
I just trained a model--

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.980
machine-learning model--
based on a public data set--

00:17:17.980 --> 00:17:20.440
the New York City
taxicab data set--

00:17:20.440 --> 00:17:22.030
to predict taxi fare.

00:17:22.030 --> 00:17:25.550
So I said, there's different
types of public data

00:17:25.550 --> 00:17:27.940
sets available on Google Cloud.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:29.230
I spent about 15 minutes.

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.760
I tracked on BigQuery.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:33.680
So let's now talk about
what else is available.

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:36.220
So we started talking about
three types of public data.

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:38.350
The second type of
public data that's

00:17:38.350 --> 00:17:42.130
available on Google Cloud
is the Google genomics data.

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:45.020
So genomics data
consists of two parts.

00:17:45.020 --> 00:17:47.110
You have some data
sets in BigQuery.

00:17:47.110 --> 00:17:49.240
These are things like
variants, et cetera.

00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:52.120
You have another data
set on Cloud Storage.

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:55.460
These are like the BAM,
VCF files, and so on.

00:17:55.460 --> 00:17:57.860
And there is a link if
you want to learn more.

00:17:57.860 --> 00:18:00.220
But one of the things
that you could do

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:05.660
is that you could actually
go ahead and use BigQuery.

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:09.500
You could use BigQuery to
go ahead and run a query

00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:13.970
like this, where you are
joining against your own table

00:18:13.970 --> 00:18:19.910
against a thousand genome data
set, for example, to figure out

00:18:19.910 --> 00:18:21.930
something about the variance.

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:23.930
And I won't embarrass
myself by talking too much

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:26.510
more about genomics, because I
don't know too much about it.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:29.180
But there is a data set
where you can actually

00:18:29.180 --> 00:18:36.260
compare a particular genome
that you have against one

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:37.960
of the public genomes.

00:18:37.960 --> 00:18:40.210
The third type of
public data set that you

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:42.970
have are geospatial data sets.

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.400
Geospatial data sets
like Landsat images,

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:47.920
or NEXRAD images,
these are images

00:18:47.920 --> 00:18:51.020
that are present on
Google Cloud Storage--

00:18:51.020 --> 00:18:53.770
so not in BitQuery anymore,
but in Google Cloud storage.

00:18:53.770 --> 00:18:55.630
In some cases a metadata.

00:18:55.630 --> 00:18:56.950
What files are available?

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:59.530
What times are
available, et cetera,

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:01.300
are present in BigQuery.

00:19:01.300 --> 00:19:04.660
But the actual data
themselves-- the raster data--

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:06.580
is on Cloud Storage.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:09.530
And also we have, of course,
the Earth Engine data sets,

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:13.410
which is another huge amount
of geospatial data sets

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:15.672
that are available
for use as well.

00:19:15.672 --> 00:19:16.880
So what is the point of this?

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:19.660
So I said initially
that public data sets

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:22.460
are about bringing
computer to the data.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.210
And I showed that with
the case of BigQuery,

00:19:25.210 --> 00:19:27.100
where we had these
large data sets,

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:31.180
and we were able to do SQL
queries on that data set.

00:19:31.180 --> 00:19:33.940
Well, when you look at
geospatial data sets,

00:19:33.940 --> 00:19:35.980
it's exactly the same way.

00:19:35.980 --> 00:19:38.680
You have your public data
sets in cloud storage.

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:43.070
And you will be able to process
them with that data in place.

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:45.070
But this time, the
processing typically

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.770
happens in Cloud Dataflow.

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:50.260
Cloud Dataflow is an
execution environment

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.840
for Apache Beam pipelines.

00:19:52.840 --> 00:19:55.220
And you can write
Apache Beam code

00:19:55.220 --> 00:19:57.130
either in Python or in Java.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:00.040
So you have two SDKs, two APIs.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:06.220
So you write your code in
Apache Beam Python or Java.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.590
And you run it in
Cloud Dataflow.

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:11.890
So it's loosely like how
you write standard SQL.

00:20:11.890 --> 00:20:13.510
And you run it in BigQuery.

00:20:13.510 --> 00:20:18.410
So you do Apache Beam, and
you run it in Dataflow.

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:22.120
And as an example,
so hurricanes are

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:25.010
in the news today in the
US, with Florence just

00:20:25.010 --> 00:20:27.170
having been here.

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:30.260
So let's look at an earlier
hurricane, Hurricane Maria.

00:20:30.260 --> 00:20:32.870
And I'll basically
show you how to process

00:20:32.870 --> 00:20:37.130
weather data in real time
in BigQuery and in Dataflow.

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:43.340
So here is a blog post.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.450
And I'll just walk
through it a little bit.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:48.560
So there is a BigQuery
public data set

00:20:48.560 --> 00:20:50.600
that consists of
NOAA hurricanes-- all

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:53.540
of the hurricanes
all over the world.

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:55.040
Basically you can
go ahead and get

00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:59.270
the latitudes, the longitude,
the time that they were.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:01.940
And in this case
I'm saying, find me

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:07.600
a hurricane whose name has
Maria in it, and from 2017,

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:09.450
and order it by ISO time.

00:21:09.450 --> 00:21:13.340
So what I'm going to now get is
the full path of the hurricane.

00:21:13.340 --> 00:21:16.790
So I have the latitude and
longitude that Maria was in,

00:21:16.790 --> 00:21:20.870
and the time at which the
hurricane was at that location,

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:25.040
and how far away from landfall
was that particular hurricane.

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:28.270
So from this time
time step, what

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.860
I'm going to then do is
I'm going to go ahead

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:37.790
and look inside the Google
Cloud public data set.

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:41.690
I can go and say, find
out my satellite data

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:44.750
for that particular year
for that particular date.

00:21:44.750 --> 00:21:48.800
And we can go ahead and pull up
the appropriate satellite data.

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:52.619
So we can go ahead, go
to this public bucket.

00:21:52.619 --> 00:21:55.160
So this is a public bucket, and
this is where the public data

00:21:55.160 --> 00:21:56.150
set comes in.

00:21:56.150 --> 00:21:59.450
And we can pull up that
particular time stamp.

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:03.500
And having done that, this
is in a format called NetCDF.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:07.310
And you have Python
code or Java libraries

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:08.790
to be able to read it.

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:14.870
So in this case, I'm
using the Python library

00:22:14.870 --> 00:22:16.580
to read NetCDF files.

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:18.800
And I'm reading those,
and I'm plotting

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:20.750
those using Matplotlib.

00:22:20.750 --> 00:22:23.870
So you can basically
go from BigQuery,

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:26.760
figure out what it is
that you want to access,

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:28.940
access the data using Python--

00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.252
it's just a data
in cloud storage.

00:22:31.252 --> 00:22:32.960
And you can do all of
the kinds of things

00:22:32.960 --> 00:22:35.090
that you would normally
do with satellite data.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:37.130
What gets interesting,
though, is

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:40.680
if you want to process large
amounts of data all at once.

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:43.200
And so that's where the next
part of this is showing.

00:22:43.200 --> 00:22:46.040
So this is now an
Apache Beam pipeline.

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:49.430
What I'm saying, go ahead
and run this hurricane query.

00:22:49.430 --> 00:22:52.790
And for every row of
the hurricane query,

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:57.260
find me all of the
[? ghost ?] files--

00:22:57.260 --> 00:22:59.540
the satellite data files--

00:22:59.540 --> 00:23:03.080
at this particular latitude and
at this particular longitude,

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:04.940
and convert them into JPEG.

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:10.340
So at that point, I will
then get on Dataflow

00:23:10.340 --> 00:23:12.875
an auto-scaling
pipeline that processes.

00:23:12.875 --> 00:23:16.460
And in this case, it's
getting all of the hurricanes

00:23:16.460 --> 00:23:17.570
everywhere.

00:23:17.570 --> 00:23:20.750
And then basically
creating a separate loop

00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:23.330
for every one of the hurricanes,
a kind of distributed

00:23:23.330 --> 00:23:25.820
processing that
allows you to process

00:23:25.820 --> 00:23:29.840
terabytes of data at scale
over hundreds of machines

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:30.780
if you need to.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:35.000
And this is the plot, for
example, of Hurricane Maria.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.190
And the other
thing you can do is

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:40.340
that you can also
listen to Pub/Sub, which

00:23:40.340 --> 00:23:41.660
is a real-time data feature.

00:23:41.660 --> 00:23:45.170
Every time a new satellite
image hits cloud storage,

00:23:45.170 --> 00:23:47.420
we publish an
event onto Pub/Sub.

00:23:47.420 --> 00:23:51.860
So you can go into Pub/Sub, and
you can listen for that Pub/Sub

00:23:51.860 --> 00:23:52.640
message.

00:23:52.640 --> 00:23:54.600
And you can do some processing.

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:57.490
So what I'm doing here is I'm
doing very simple processing.

00:23:57.490 --> 00:24:00.000
I'm basically going
ahead and cropping out

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:03.145
a part of the image that
corresponds to Seattle.

00:24:03.145 --> 00:24:05.270
And I'm basically saying,
here is a satellite image

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:07.950
over Seattle at this
current point in time.

00:24:07.950 --> 00:24:10.160
But as with the
hurricane data, you

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.230
can do this for
every city in the US.

00:24:12.230 --> 00:24:14.210
You can do it for
different areas.

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:15.350
You can create loops.

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:17.180
You can do all of
those kinds of things.

00:24:17.180 --> 00:24:19.471
And the cool thing is you
can do them all in real time.

00:24:24.110 --> 00:24:31.070
So with that, I'll basically
leave you with some resources.

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:33.220
First of all, shameless
plug for my book.

00:24:33.220 --> 00:24:36.260
If you're interested in doing
data analysis data science

00:24:36.260 --> 00:24:39.522
machine learning
on Google Cloud,

00:24:39.522 --> 00:24:41.980
there is a O'Reilly book, "Data
Science on the Google Cloud

00:24:41.980 --> 00:24:44.410
Platform," written
by yours truly.

00:24:44.410 --> 00:24:48.040
The second thing is
that we have a number

00:24:48.040 --> 00:24:49.810
of courses on Coursera.

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:51.940
And if you're
watching this webinar,

00:24:51.940 --> 00:24:57.910
go to coursera.org/NEXTExtended,
and you will get one month

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:00.160
of courses free on the platform.

00:25:00.160 --> 00:25:02.260
So please take advantage of it.

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:04.360
And finally, if
you go to Qwiklabs,

00:25:04.360 --> 00:25:07.630
there's a variety of labs,
including many of the things

00:25:07.630 --> 00:25:09.670
that I demonstrated,
[? RS ?] labs

00:25:09.670 --> 00:25:13.510
that you can try out yourself.

00:25:13.510 --> 00:25:16.210
And that's a wrap.

00:25:16.210 --> 00:25:19.590
Stay tuned for live Q&amp;A. We'll
be back in about a minute.

00:25:24.300 --> 00:25:26.220
Welcome back.

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:28.830
So I'm taking
questions right now.

00:25:28.830 --> 00:25:30.990
So if you have more
questions, please add them

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:32.380
to the platform.

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:36.570
So first question, how often
are these public data sets

00:25:36.570 --> 00:25:38.640
updated to reflect new data?

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:40.300
Great question.

00:25:40.300 --> 00:25:41.470
There are some data sets.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:44.940
For example the satellite
data that I just showed you.

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:48.760
That satellite data
streams into the cloud.

00:25:48.760 --> 00:25:54.000
And so we actually have
new data every time

00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:56.860
there's a new image that's
downloaded from the satellite.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:26:00.070
So if you're taking over a full
disk, it's every 15 minutes.

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.430
But lots of times you have
new data every minute.

00:26:02.430 --> 00:26:09.120
So it is near real time for
cases like Landsat data,

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.240
for cases like Sentinel, for
cases like the [? ghost ?]

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:13.020
data.

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:16.210
For the BigQuery data sets,
also if you're thinking,

00:26:16.210 --> 00:26:19.920
for example, the
city bikes data,

00:26:19.920 --> 00:26:25.380
or the [? ghost ?] temperature,
or the [? gsort ?] temperature

00:26:25.380 --> 00:26:28.420
data, the weather data, all
of those kinds of things,

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:33.180
as and when new data is
available from the public data

00:26:33.180 --> 00:26:38.400
set provider, that data is
reflected on Google Cloud

00:26:38.400 --> 00:26:39.100
as well.

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:41.710
So the answer to how often
are these public data

00:26:41.710 --> 00:26:46.200
sets updated, it is like we
try to keep them as up to date

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:47.920
as we can.

00:26:47.920 --> 00:26:50.340
Some data sets, like
the zip codes data set,

00:26:50.340 --> 00:26:54.090
we might update once a
year, the utility data sets.

00:26:54.090 --> 00:26:57.490
But for the most part,
as a rule of thumb,

00:26:57.490 --> 00:27:00.000
the public data sets
are as up to date

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:04.680
as the provider of the public
data sets allows us to be.

00:27:04.680 --> 00:27:06.570
Second question.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:09.480
How do you decide
which data sets

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:13.120
to feature on your
public data sets program?

00:27:13.120 --> 00:27:14.940
This is where you can help.

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.270
If you have suggestions
on what data

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:18.990
sets we should be
featuring, data

00:27:18.990 --> 00:27:24.060
sets that you would find
useful, please do let us know.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.360
And remember that even though
I talked about public data sets

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:31.050
on Google Cloud, there
is a lot of public data

00:27:31.050 --> 00:27:32.130
available out there.

00:27:32.130 --> 00:27:33.870
There is data on Earth Engine.

00:27:33.870 --> 00:27:35.960
There's data on Kaggle.

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:42.690
And for, to some extent, our
goal with the Google Cloud

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:45.720
public data sets program
is to focus on data

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:51.880
sets where it's large enough,
or it's useful enough that--

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:53.710
So, number one, it's very large.

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:55.530
So you need to bring
compute to the data.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:59.040
So we want to basically feature
it to make it easier for folks.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:01.470
The second reason is that
it's a very useful data set.

00:28:01.470 --> 00:28:04.560
Like the zip code data
set is not very large.

00:28:04.560 --> 00:28:07.110
But it is an extremely
useful data set.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:09.630
So we will have it as
a utility data set,

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:15.450
so each individual company
doesn't have to do that.

00:28:15.450 --> 00:28:17.280
So we either do it
as a public good,

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:20.460
or we do it as because it's
a very large computing, even

00:28:20.460 --> 00:28:23.750
though genomics data is
not very widely used,

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:25.890
but it's very useful,
and it's very large.

00:28:25.890 --> 00:28:27.340
So we tend to feature it.

00:28:27.340 --> 00:28:31.800
And so the way we decide
is please let us know.

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:33.960
And if you're a
provider of data,

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:38.160
and you would like your data
to be featured on Google Cloud,

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:39.620
also do contact us.

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:40.800
There's two ways to do it.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:42.370
We can do the public data set.

00:28:42.370 --> 00:28:46.590
But we can also do the Google
Cloud Marketplace, which allows

00:28:46.590 --> 00:28:50.142
you to monetize your data set.

00:28:50.142 --> 00:28:51.930
Third question.

00:28:51.930 --> 00:28:54.360
You showed a number of links.

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.550
How can I get a
copy of your slides

00:28:56.550 --> 00:28:58.650
so that I can visit
those websites?

00:28:58.650 --> 00:29:00.370
Good point.

00:29:00.370 --> 00:29:02.670
If you stick around
in the Q&amp;A, we

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:06.600
will post a link
to my presentation

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:08.280
so that you can
actually get access.

00:29:08.280 --> 00:29:10.840
And then you can
go to that link.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:13.140
And then all of the other
links are available.

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:15.030
So feel free to explore.

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:17.160
My deck has lots
and lots of links.

00:29:17.160 --> 00:29:21.380
And each of them is
a great set of things

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:22.380
to go ahead and explore.

00:29:22.380 --> 00:29:26.170
So strongly encourage
you to do that.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:28.000
Fourth question.

00:29:28.000 --> 00:29:29.020
That's a cool one.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:32.350
BigQuery and machine
learning seems very cool.

00:29:32.350 --> 00:29:35.470
When should I use BigQuery
ML versus TensorFlow?

00:29:35.470 --> 00:29:36.820
That's a great question.

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:39.760
So I just showed
you BigQuery ML.

00:29:39.760 --> 00:29:41.650
A little bit of SQL,
and you basically

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:43.390
have a machine-learning model.

00:29:43.390 --> 00:29:44.900
It's amazing.

00:29:44.900 --> 00:29:48.020
So what's all this thing about
this whole TensorFlow stuff?

00:29:48.020 --> 00:29:49.120
Do we need it?

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.130
What is it about?

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.880
Well, the thing to
realize is, number one,

00:29:51.880 --> 00:29:54.760
BigQuery ML is about
structured data.

00:29:54.760 --> 00:30:00.220
And in many ways, if your
data set is very large,

00:30:00.220 --> 00:30:01.810
and it's very sparse--

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:05.270
in other words, there's a
lot of categorical columns--

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:09.340
BigQuery ML will be very
competitive with a TensorFlow

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:09.970
model.

00:30:09.970 --> 00:30:13.240
So BigQuery ML would be
great for large data sets

00:30:13.240 --> 00:30:15.970
with lots of columns,
and especially

00:30:15.970 --> 00:30:19.420
lots of categorical columns
that have lots of values--

00:30:19.420 --> 00:30:21.310
what I'll call a
sparse data set.

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:25.760
For all other types of data,
TensorFlow is a great choice.

00:30:25.760 --> 00:30:28.850
So if you have image data,
you have time series data,

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:31.000
you have dense
data sets, you have

00:30:31.000 --> 00:30:33.580
data sets that involve a
mixture of these things,

00:30:33.580 --> 00:30:37.840
TensorFlow is a much more
flexible, powerful framework.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:40.540
The way I like to
think of BigQuery ML

00:30:40.540 --> 00:30:44.740
is that BigQuery ML is a
great way by which, rather

00:30:44.740 --> 00:30:48.770
than writing TensorFlow code to
write a machine learning model,

00:30:48.770 --> 00:30:52.220
you get to express your
machine learning model in SQL.

00:30:52.220 --> 00:30:57.430
So think of BigQuery as
SQL is the user interface.

00:30:57.430 --> 00:31:01.630
And in the future I
can easily imagine

00:31:01.630 --> 00:31:04.540
a world in which you
write your model in SQL,

00:31:04.540 --> 00:31:07.300
but it doesn't get
executed in BigQuery ML.

00:31:07.300 --> 00:31:11.560
Instead, it gets executed in
TensorFlow on Cloud ML engine.

00:31:11.560 --> 00:31:15.250
But the way you express
your model could be a SQL.

00:31:15.250 --> 00:31:18.340
So maybe you want to do
the separation between SQL

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:21.110
as a UI, which is very,
very, very attractive;

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:24.580
and SQL as the execution
engine, which may or may not

00:31:24.580 --> 00:31:27.050
be limited in what it can do.

00:31:27.050 --> 00:31:28.500
But I agree with you.

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:32.170
BigQuery ML is super cool.

00:31:32.170 --> 00:31:35.130
Final question, looks like.

00:31:35.130 --> 00:31:39.010
Are public data sets available
to be used on other cloud

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:41.290
providers' platform also?

00:31:41.290 --> 00:31:42.700
A good question.

00:31:42.700 --> 00:31:46.030
So the public sets are public.

00:31:46.030 --> 00:31:50.600
So, many of them,
you can access them.

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:52.650
But something like
a BigQuery data

00:31:52.650 --> 00:31:55.900
set, you would have
to transfer the data.

00:31:55.900 --> 00:31:59.350
I cannot imagine how you
would be able to use that from

00:31:59.350 --> 00:32:00.790
another cloud provider.

00:32:00.790 --> 00:32:05.740
On the other hand, the Google
Cloud Storage data sets,

00:32:05.740 --> 00:32:06.850
you can absolutely.

00:32:06.850 --> 00:32:10.880
You can access it from another
cloud service provider.

00:32:10.880 --> 00:32:12.880
But typically there
might be an egress fee

00:32:12.880 --> 00:32:15.850
involved, because you're
actually crossing a network.

00:32:15.850 --> 00:32:17.680
But other than that, yes.

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:20.230
So if there is a public
data set on Google Cloud,

00:32:20.230 --> 00:32:25.120
on Cloud Storage, and you have
your code in some other cloud

00:32:25.120 --> 00:32:28.360
service provider, you should
be able to access it the way

00:32:28.360 --> 00:32:31.790
you would access any
other data on GCP.

00:32:31.790 --> 00:32:32.890
So it is an open platform.

00:32:32.890 --> 00:32:34.297
You will be able to access it.

00:32:34.297 --> 00:32:35.630
And you should be able to do it.

00:32:35.630 --> 00:32:37.330
So even if it isn't BigQuery.

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:41.150
And now one option might be to
basically run an extraction,

00:32:41.150 --> 00:32:45.670
or run a query to extract a part
of the data set that you care

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:49.390
about, and then transfer that
data over, and process it

00:32:49.390 --> 00:32:52.670
somewhere else on prem or in
some other cloud provider.

00:32:55.370 --> 00:32:57.970
Any other questions?

00:32:57.970 --> 00:32:58.960
All right.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:02.200
And I guess that's a wrap.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:04.300
Stay tuned for
the next session--

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:08.920
[? CETV-- ?] Application
Observability with Light Step.

00:33:08.920 --> 00:33:10.330
Thank you very much.

